+# BLOCKED: exact median in O(1) memory
+
+## Status
+
+- Fixed: `median([1, 2, 3, 4])` returned `3`; it now returns `2.5` (mean of the
+  two middle values). Odd, empty, and generator cases still pass.
+- Blocked: `test_constant_memory` requires an **exact** median of a
+  single-pass stream in **under 64 KiB**, and that is not possible in general.
+
+Current measured peak for the test feed (200,000 floats) is **7,223,968 bytes
+(~7.05 MiB)** against the 64 KiB limit. Correctness of the value now matches
+the test's expected `(exact[99_999] + exact[100_000]) / 2`.
+
+## Why it is impossible
+
+The input is an arbitrary single-pass iterable and the result must be exact.
+
+- Any exact one-pass algorithm that selects the median of `n` arbitrary values
+  needs Ω(n) bits of state. This is the classic lower bound for selection with
+  limited storage (Munro & Paterson, *Selection and sorting with limited
+  storage*, 1980); the adversary can force the algorithm to retain enough of
+  the input to distinguish inputs whose medians differ.
+- Concretely, `n` 64-bit floats carry up to 64n bits of information. With a
+  64 KiB budget only ~512K bits are available, far less than the 12.8M bits in
+  the test's 200,000 values. A fixed-size summary (histogram, sketch, sample)
+  must therefore lose information, so it cannot guarantee the exact float
+  returned by the test.
+- The bound does not come from Python's `sorted` in particular. No container,
+  selection algorithm, or incremental data structure changes it; `sorted` is
+  simply the straightforward Ω(n)-space exact implementation.
+
+## Why the usual "workarounds" are not valid here
+
+Per the task, I did not work around the impossibility. In particular:
+
+- **External/temp-file selection**: spilling the stream to disk and doing
+  multi-pass selection keeps RAM low but still uses O(n) storage. It also has
+  to consume the entire generator up front, so it is not constant memory in
+  the intended sense.
+- **Approximate streaming medians** (P², t-digest, reservoir sampling,
+  histograms) use bounded memory but are not exact, and this test asserts
+  equality with the exact float.
+- **Fixed-domain counting**: could be O(domain) memory, but not O(1), and the
+  feed contains arbitrary Gaussian floats; matching the exact expected value
+  would still require retaining the original values.
+- **Multi-pass refinement**: would be exact if the input were re-iterable and
+  could be narrowed each pass, but `feed()` is a one-shot generator.
+
+## What would unblock this
+
+One of the following requirement changes is needed:
+
+1. Accept a bounded **approximation** (with an explicit error/rank guarantee)
+   instead of exact equality, or
+2. Supply a **re-iterable/seekable** input so an exact multi-pass selection
+   algorithm can run in O(1) working memory, or
+3. Allow O(n) auxiliary storage (memory or disk) for exact results, as the
+   current implementation does.
+
+Until then `test_constant_memory` remains failing by design, and the exact
+implementation stays O(n) in space.
